gm-method/rendering (tree 'cull on approach' in a three InstancedMesh2 + LOD + per-instance-BVH veg system, two-part lesson): (1) ROOT CAUSE & FIX of genuine cull-on-approach: per-LOD SIMPLIFIED geometries each auto-compute their OWN partial boundingBox/boundingSphere; the per-instance BVH (InstancedMeshBVH reads geometry.boundingBox at construction) then culls the instance when the camera nears and the partial box exits the frustum. FIX: compute the full-tree union box = branchGeo0.boundingBox.union(leafGeo0.boundingBox), getBoundingSphere, and STAMP that identical union box+sphere on the parent geos AND every addLOD child geo of BOTH the branch and leaf InstancedMesh2 (incl defensive stamp of mesh.children[*]._geometry) BEFORE construction, so every cull path tests the whole tree. (2) DEBUGGING DISCIPLINE that prevented 4+ false positives: a 'canopy vanished' or 'bare trunk' frame is NOT a cull until isolated. The decisive isolation is the 3-STATE VISIBILITY TOGGLE: screenshot both-on, then leaf-mesh-OFF (branch only), then branch-mesh-OFF (leaf only); diffing the three proves whether leaves actually render at that distance. This refuted an apparent 'leaves missing on distant poles' that was really thin white-bark trunks out-shining sparse high canopies against bright sky. Also: 'canopy gone looking up' was the camera pitched at empty SKY (verify with asin(forward.y) and project box corners), and LODinfo.render.count is per-NEAREST-instance not whole-mesh. Always pair a count/visibility probe (getVisibilityAt, frustum.intersectsBox on geo.boundingBox*instanceMatrix, asin(fwd.y) pitch) with a screenshot before claiming any instanced-LOD cull. The TPS camera rig RECLAIMS camera.position AND cam.pitch every frame, so setting them then awaiting rAF does nothing; drive REAL KeyboardEvent(KeyW)+MouseEvent(movementY) input instead.